From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] VU0 macro mode: VDIV, VSQRT and VRSQRT as the VU interpreters

What it broke:
  - VRSQRT computed 1 / sqrt(ft.ftf) and ignored fs.fsf: RSQRT Q, fs, ft
    is fs / sqrt(ft) (6 / sqrt(9) gave 0.333 instead of 2).
  - VDIV by zero gave Q = 0; VRSQRT and VSQRT of a negative value gave 0.
    PCSX2's VU interpreter (pcsx2/VUops.cpp _vuDIV, _vuSQRT, _vuRSQRT)
    and this runtime's own VU interpreter give +-0x7F7FFFFF for a zero
    divisor (sign of fs xor ft) and use |ft| for the root.
  - Operands with exponent 0 or 255 were used as host floats.
  Protector of Earth's 124 VRSQRT and 56 VDIV all use vf0.w (1.0) as fs,
  so only their zero and negative cases changed.

The changes:
  - ps2_runtime_macros.h: ps2_vu_double (PCSX2 vuDouble: exponent 0 ->
    +-0, exponent 255 -> +-0x7F7FFFFF), ps2_vu0_div, ps2_vu0_sqrt and
    ps2_vu0_rsqrt, following _vuDIV/_vuSQRT/_vuRSQRT (status flags not
    kept; the macro-mode context has none).
  - vu_translation_helpers.cpp: VDIV, VSQRT and VRSQRT call them.

How it was checked:
  New per-instruction cases (findings/14 s.8): VdivSetsQ, VsqrtIgnoresSign
  and VrsqrtDividesFs failed before (Q 0 for x/0, 0 for sqrt(-16), 0.333
  for 6 / sqrt(9)) and pass after; the 8 other new VU0 macro cases (VMULx,
  VADDbc, VSUB, VMUL, VMAX, VMINIx, the ACC matrix chain, VOPMULA/VOPMSUB,
  VMR32, VFTOI0/VITOF12) pass before and after. ps2xTest 454/454.

